WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.277
In this film you take a FundsXML delivery apart: its main blocks, and its control data, the envelope of every delivery.

2
00:00:08.327 --> 00:00:10.119
It takes about five minutes.

3
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:19.634
Here is a realistic FundsXML delivery: the month-end data of a fictional multi-asset fund, from the public example files.

4
00:00:19.684 --> 00:00:23.523
Every document starts with the root element FundsXML4.

5
00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:27.844
It has no namespace, and it names its schema, FundsXML4.xsd.

6
00:00:29.122 --> 00:00:33.384
Fold the document to its main parts, and its structure becomes clear.

7
00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:35.009
This file has four blocks.

8
00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:37.634
ControlData describes the delivery itself.

9
00:00:37.684 --> 00:00:41.946
Funds holds the funds, with their static data, prices and portfolios.

10
00:00:41.996 --> 00:00:46.071
AssetMasterData describes every security that the portfolios hold.

11
00:00:46.121 --> 00:00:52.008
And RegulatoryReportings carries templates for partners, here an EMT and a Tripartite Template.

12
00:00:53.158 --> 00:00:55.329
These are the blocks at the top level.

13
00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:57.958
ControlData is the only one that is required.

14
00:00:58.008 --> 00:01:01.114
Funds and AssetMasterData carry the classic fund data.

15
00:01:01.164 --> 00:01:07.426
A block for the management company holds figures of the company itself, such as its assets under management.

16
00:01:07.476 --> 00:01:12.335
Documents links fund documents, such as key information documents or annual reports.

17
00:01:12.385 --> 00:01:15.374
RegulatoryReportings carries the European templates.

18
00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:18.004
CountrySpecificData holds national reporting.

19
00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:23.732
And an optional signature lets the receiver check who sent the file, and that nothing was changed.

20
00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:33.258
A delivery contains only the blocks it needs: a price feed has funds but no portfolio, and a pure template delivery may have little more than its regulatory block.

21
00:01:34.508 --> 00:01:36.329
Now the envelope, ControlData.

22
00:01:36.379 --> 00:01:41.944
The unique document ID identifies this delivery; it must be unique for each data supplier.

23
00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:45.874
Document generated is the time stamp when the file was written.

24
00:01:45.924 --> 00:01:49.243
Version names the FundsXML version it was written for.

25
00:01:49.293 --> 00:01:54.483
And content date is the business date of the data, here the thirty-first of October.

26
00:01:54.533 --> 00:02:00.722
Do not confuse the two dates: the content date is what the data refers to, not when it was produced.

27
00:02:02.091 --> 00:02:10.923
The data supplier says who sent the file: the country of the sending system, a short name, the full name, and the type of supplier, here an asset manager.

28
00:02:11.573 --> 00:02:13.773
The last field is the data operation.

29
00:02:13.823 --> 00:02:16.084
Initial is a first, complete delivery.

30
00:02:16.134 --> 00:02:25.753
A click into it, and the Properties panel shows the schema's own explanation: amend updates or corrects data that was already delivered, and delete removes it.

31
00:02:26.903 --> 00:02:29.214
In practice, deliveries form a chain.

32
00:02:29.264 --> 00:02:30.809
The first one is initial.

33
00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:40.063
A correction is a new file, with its own unique document ID, the operation amend, and the ID of the delivery it corrects in related document IDs.

34
00:02:40.113 --> 00:02:44.594
Delete works the same way, and always names the documents it refers to.

35
00:02:44.644 --> 00:02:49.891
So the receiver can follow every change, from the first delivery to the latest one.

36
00:02:51.141 --> 00:02:53.908
Let us turn this delivery into a correction.

37
00:02:53.958 --> 00:02:58.965
The document gets a new ID: the old one with a suffix for the first correction.

38
00:02:59.015 --> 00:03:00.757
The operation becomes amend.

39
00:03:00.807 --> 00:03:05.559
And related document IDs name the delivery it corrects, by its original ID.

40
00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:13.433
Press F8 to validate: the file is still valid, and the receiver now knows exactly which earlier delivery this one replaces.

41
00:03:14.582 --> 00:03:15.843
Some common pitfalls.

42
00:03:15.893 --> 00:03:20.149
Reusing a document ID: the receiver may drop the file as a duplicate.

43
00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:23.893
Mixing up the two dates, so data is booked on the wrong day.

44
00:03:23.943 --> 00:03:28.573
A version that does not match the schema that the file was checked against.

45
00:03:28.623 --> 00:03:33.565
And corrections without related document IDs, which leave the receiver guessing.

46
00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:37.297
This file uses only a part of the standard.

47
00:03:37.347 --> 00:03:41.654
Open the schema in the graphic view: here are all blocks at the top level.

48
00:03:41.704 --> 00:03:52.664
Regulatory reportings is opened: it splits into direct reporting, and indirect reporting with the templates for partners, such as the Tripartite Template in its versions, and PRIIPs data.

49
00:03:52.714 --> 00:03:59.965
Every block can be explored like this, down to the last element, with the documentation of each one in the Properties panel.

50
00:04:01.117 --> 00:04:03.385
That was the anatomy of a delivery.

51
00:04:03.435 --> 00:04:08.353
In the next film, you look at funds, share classes and the net asset value.

52
00:04:08.403 --> 00:04:09.678
Thanks for watching.
